      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5d4d69781ad8ea1fc151fb20",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Security is always going to be a major issue due to the basic architecture of the game. As long as the community at large doesn't condemn the use of the tools that allow you to do these things, it won't grow.\n\nIn the mean time, it's better to educate yourself as to what the third party programs allow users to do, so as to better defend yourself. For example, most clients merely allow you to clone any public avatar at any time, or save more favorites. More advanced clients usually also allow you to timeout specific players. \n\nOfficial answers will be sparse, due to the minefield that is addressing security in a social platform. Personally, I'll never use a third party client, as I don't see the need to, and I discourage everyone I know from using them as well.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "5d62313e4959263e17873239",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5af8fc26a97e4b511e461b1d",
        "alias": "Juniper Lungfish",
        "aliasID": "65c26da3ed6eefce4c4d093f",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/c09b1c5d29115d43ef850e6d223e6851.png",
        "created": "2018-05-14T03:01:58.085Z",
        "name": "Legoman99573",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2019-08-25T06:57:02.955Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5d4d69781ad8ea1fc151fb20",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "When a player switches an avatar, no matter public or private, they still get the avatar id from output logs, which they pull the avatar file from the API and rip it that way. That cannot be fixed however as game rips exist with unity games in general, and I just accepted the fact that someone will rip my avatar for some sort of asset and try to sell it as their own.\n\nThe DoS/DDoS attacks only occur because the malicious user can grab your IP from the Steam API, which happens to all steam games and VRChat is no exception to this happening. Using a VPN or closing steam completely and launch VRChat.exe from the folder itself (meaning going to task manager and killing steam background proccess) should make it a little harder for them to get your IP really easily.\n\nCrashing is the reason the safety settings exist, but the worst is usually shaders, with the particle system can disable playerlocal if you setup the limiter.\n\nSome things I do believe can be fixed, while some things will happen no matter what as people will find a loophole where the devs made flaws with the systems and users exploit it as much as possible until it gets patched or never patched.\n\nHere is what can be fixed: \n* adding a shader whitelist/blacklist or adding a shaders folder and allow you to put in the shaders manually to load that shader over another malicious shader using a common name\n* prevent getting the avatar id by removing it from logs (essentially making it a little more challenging to get the id of the avatar)\n\nHere is what can't really be fixed (but i can be wrong on some): \n* People grabbing your IP from the SteamAPI with malicious clients\n* Ripping Avatars/Assets (as most rippers were made for unity games)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
